The Security Council,
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,
Recalling its resolutions 2118 (2013), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2254 (2015), 2258 (2015), 2332 (2016), 2393 (2017) and 2401 (2018), and the relevant statements of its President,
Reaffirming that the use of chemical weapons, wherever, by whomever and under whatever circumstances, is unacceptable and constitutes a violation of international law and a threat to international peace and security,
Recalling the obligations of all States parties under the Convention on the Prohibition of the Development, Production, Stockpiling and Use of Chemical Weapons and on Their Destruction, and the obligations of the Syrian Arab Republic under resolution 2118 (2013),
Deeply alarmed by the reports of alleged chemical weapons use in Douma, Syrian Arab Republic, on 7 April 2018, and deploring the reported loss of life,
Stressing the need to establish the facts promptly, independently and impartially, and taking note of the invitation extended by the Government of the Syrian Arab Republic to the OPCW to visit Douma and of the OPCW’s decision to deploy a Fact-Finding Mission to Douma,
Stressing the importance of full cooperation with the OPCW to ensure a thorough, objective and credible investigation, and stressing also that the integrity and independence of the OPCW’s work must be preserved,
Emphasizing that all parties must refrain from any action that could undermine the credibility of the OPCW investigation or prejudge its outcome,
Expressing grave concern at the humanitarian situation in Syria, including in Eastern Ghouta, Idlib, Raqqa, Rukban and other areas, and reaffirming its demand for full implementation of resolution 2401 (2018),
Reaffirming that there can be no military solution to the conflict and that the only sustainable solution is through an inclusive Syrian-led and Syrian-owned political process facilitated by the United Nations, in accordance with resolution 2254 (2015),
Reaffirming the need to combat terrorism, including ISIL/Da’esh, Al Qaeda and associated groups, and stressing that any measures taken must comply with international law,
Determining that the situation in Syria continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security,
1. Condemns any use of chemical weapons and calls upon all parties to refrain from any use of chemical weapons and to comply fully with their obligations under international law;
2. Takes note of the reports of alleged chemical weapons use in Douma on 7 April 2018 and stresses the need for a full, objective and independent investigation by the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission in accordance with OPCW procedures;
3. Calls upon all parties to cooperate fully with the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission, including by ensuring safe, unhindered and immediate access to all relevant sites and persons, and to ensure the safety and security of OPCW personnel;
4. Calls upon all parties and all Member States to refrain from any acts that could prejudge the outcome of the OPCW investigation, and to avoid any further escalation that could complicate the situation;
5. Underlines the importance of holding accountable those responsible for any use of chemical weapons, and emphasizes that any determination of responsibility must be based on verifiable evidence and established through appropriate procedures;
6. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council, in coordination with the OPCW, on the findings of the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission as soon as possible and within 30 days, and to keep the Council informed of the implementation of this resolution;
7. Reaffirms its demand for the full implementation of resolution 2401 (2018), including the cessation of hostilities and the safe, unimpeded and sustained delivery of humanitarian assistance throughout Syria, and calls upon all parties to facilitate humanitarian access, including to Douma and Eastern Ghouta;
8. Calls for the protection of civilians, medical personnel, humanitarian personnel and civilian objects, and reiterates the need for all parties to comply with international humanitarian law;
9. Reaffirms that the cessation of hostilities shall not apply to military operations against ISIL, Al Qaeda, Al Nusra and other terrorist groups designated by the Security Council;
10. Reaffirms its support for the Syrian-led and Syrian-owned political process under the auspices of the United Nations, in accordance with resolution 2254 (2015), and calls upon all parties to engage constructively and without preconditions;
11. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

